## Background

The NYC Department of Education asked: **what can data tell us about the
relationship between poverty and test performance in New York public
schools?** This report looks at that question using school-level test
score data from NYSED and county-level poverty data from the ACS,
2008-2017.

```{r load-data}
merged <- read_csv("data/processed/merged.csv")
county_summary <- read_csv("data/processed/county_summary.csv")
top_bottom_scores <- read_csv("data/processed/top_bottom_poverty_scores.csv")
score_range_by_year <- read_csv("data/processed/score_range_by_year.csv")

source("scripts/4_plot.R")
```

A couple of data quality notes worth surfacing to the department before
the results: about 6% of school-year math/ELA scores are missing
(coded `-99` in the raw file), and roughly 40 rows had free/reduced-lunch
percentages above 100%, which we've treated as data entry errors and
excluded rather than tried to correct.

## Table 1: County-Level Summary

Total enrollment, % free/reduced lunch, and % poverty, averaged across all
years in the data for each county. Top 10 counties by poverty rate shown
below; the full table is in `data/processed/county_summary.csv`.

```{r table-1}
county_summary |>
  slice_max(pct_poverty, n = 10) |>
  mutate(
    pct_frl = scales::percent(pct_frl, accuracy = 0.1),
    pct_poverty = scales::percent(pct_poverty, accuracy = 0.1)
  ) |>
  knitr::kable(col.names = c("County", "Total Enrollment", "% FRL", "% Poverty"))
```

## Table 2: Top 5 vs. Bottom 5 Poverty Counties

```{r table-2}
top_bottom_scores |>
  mutate(
    pct_frl = scales::percent(pct_frl, accuracy = 0.1),
    pct_poverty = scales::percent(pct_poverty, accuracy = 0.1),
    mean_math = round(mean_math, 1),
    mean_ela = round(mean_ela, 1)
  ) |>
  select(poverty_rank, county_name, pct_poverty, pct_frl, mean_math, mean_ela) |>
  arrange(poverty_rank) |>
  knitr::kable(col.names = c("Group", "County", "% Poverty", "% FRL", "Mean Math", "Mean ELA"))
```

**Takeaway:** the bottom-5 poverty counties post noticeably higher average
math and ELA scores than the top-5 poverty counties.

